    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Formed in 1230 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III</a> united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León, it took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cordoba">Córdoba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seville">Seville</a> and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#granada">Granada</a> as the last Muslim state.</li><li>Merino wool marketed by the Mesta, the Cortes and Alfonso X's Siete Partidas shaped its economy and law.</li><li>Under Isabella I it took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#granada">Granada</a> and sponsored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>, and its laws and language spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spanish-america">Spanish Americas</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Alfonsine Tables, astronomical tables compiled at the court of Alfonso X in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#toledo">Toledo</a>, remained a standard reference for European astronomers until the sixteenth century.</p></aside>
    </section>

