    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ferdinand-iii-of-castile"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile.jpg" alt="Ferdinand III of Castile" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fernando_III_de_Castilla_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> took shape in 1230, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III</a>, king of Castile since 1217, inherited the Kingdom of León on the death of his father Alfonso IX. By the Treaty of Benavente his half-sisters gave up their claims in return for pensions, and the two crowns, joined before but separated again in 1157, were never divided thereafter. The union also brought the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#galicia">Galicia</a> and León's frontier lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#extremadura">Extremadura</a>. Ferdinand turned the combined resources against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, which had fragmented after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a> in 1212. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 1236, received the submission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#murcia">Murcia</a> in 1243, and in 1246 accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassalage</a> of Muhammad I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#granada">Granada</a>, who handed over Jaén. In 1248, after a siege of more than a year supported by a fleet under Ramón Bonifaz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seville">Seville</a> surrendered. Within two decades the crown had greatly enlarged its territory, absorbing the Guadalquivir valley and leaving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emirate">Emirate</a> of Granada as the last Muslim state in the peninsula. Ferdinand III, canonised in 1671, became a model of the crusading king, and his conquests fixed a frontier with Granada that would stand, with modest changes, for more than two centuries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#medina"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/medina.jpg" alt="Medina" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#medina">Medina</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Masjid_An-Nabawi_(Bird%27s_Eye_View).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Konevi, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> was the largest and most populous kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, with perhaps four to five million people around 1500, several times the population of the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>. The conquests of the thirteenth century transferred vast estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#andalusia">Andalusia</a> to the crown, the nobility, the Church and the military orders of Santiago, Calatrava and Alcántara, laying the foundations of a powerful landed aristocracy. Pastoral farming dominated much of the Meseta: in 1273 Alfonso X chartered the Honrado Concejo de la Mesta, the association of sheep owners whose merino flocks migrated each year between summer pastures in the north and winter grazing in the south. Fine wool, shipped from the Cantabrian ports by the merchants of Burgos to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#flanders">Flanders</a>, became Castile's leading export, and the fairs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#medina">Medina</a> del Campo made it a centre of European finance. Society was religiously mixed: large Jewish communities lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#toledo">Toledo</a>, Burgos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seville">Seville</a>, and Muslims remained as Mudéjars. Anti-Jewish riots in 1391 forced mass conversions, and suspicion of the converts, or <em>conversos</em>, later fed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a>. After 1492 the crown claimed a monopoly of trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a>, organised from 1503 through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#casa-de-contratacion">Casa de Contratación</a> in Seville, and American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> flowed into Castile, enriching the crown but also fuelling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inflation">inflation</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#italian-wars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/italian-wars.jpg" alt="Italian Wars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#italian-wars">Italian Wars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italian_War_Battle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Waylon1104, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> was a society organised for frontier war. Town militias, the military orders and the great nobles provided most of its forces, and the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#granada">Granada</a> saw constant raiding even in times of truce. Alfonso XI won the decisive Battle of Río Salado in 1340 over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#morocco">Morocco</a> and Yusuf I of Granada and took Algeciras in 1344, ending large North African interventions in the peninsula, though he died of plague while besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> in 1350. The civil war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peter-the-great">Peter I</a> and his half-brother Henry of Trastámara drew in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>: Peter won the Battle of Nájera in 1367 with the help of Edward the Black Prince, but Henry killed him at Montiel in 1369. Castilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#galley">galleys</a>, allied with France, defeated an English fleet off La Rochelle in 1372, while an attempt on the Portuguese throne ended at the Battle of Aljubarrota in 1385. Isabella I and Ferdinand II fought the Granada War of 1482–1492 with large armies, siege artillery and an efficient supply system, experience that Gonzalo Fernández de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cordoba">Córdoba</a> carried into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> Castilian infantry formed the core of the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tercio">tercios</a></em> that fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#flanders">Flanders</a>, while Castilian conquistadors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> overthrew the Aztec and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inca-empire">Inca</a> empires.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#pope-sixtus-iv"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv.jpg" alt="Pope Sixtus IV" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_Berruguete_-_Portrait_of_Pope_Sixtus_IV_della_Rovere_-_1916.815_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_ArtFXD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pedro Berruguete, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> ruled without a fixed capital for most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, their court moving between Burgos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#toledo">Toledo</a>, Valladolid, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seville">Seville</a> and other cities until Philip II settled it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#madrid">Madrid</a> in 1561. Royal power was comparatively strong. Alfonso X, known as the Wise, issued the <em>Fuero Real</em> and the great legal code of the <em>Siete Partidas</em>, which gained force of law only in 1348 under Alfonso XI but became a foundation of Castilian and later Spanish American law. The Cortes, where representatives of the leading towns met with the clergy and nobility, voted taxes but, unlike the parliaments of the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, rarely restrained royal legislation, and by the sixteenth century only eighteen cities held votes. The Royal Council, reorganised in 1385 and staffed increasingly by university-trained lawyers, became the Council of Castile, beside high courts of appeal at Valladolid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#granada">Granada</a>. Isabella I and Ferdinand II restored order after decades of noble faction: they created the Santa Hermandad as a rural police force in 1476, placed royal <em>corregidores</em> in the towns, took over the masterships of the military orders and secured a decisive say in appointing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishop">bishops</a>. The Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a>, authorised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> in 1478, became a royal tribunal answerable to the crown rather than to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#christopher-columbus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus.jpg" alt="Christopher Columbus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_Man,_Said_to_be_Christopher_Columbus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sebastiano del Piombo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> was the core around which modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> was built and the launching point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>'s first great overseas empire. Its crown sponsored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#capitulations-of-the-ottoman-empire">Capitulations</a> of Santa Fe in 1492, divided the non-European world with Portugal by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a> in 1494, and treated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a> as Castilian possessions: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a>, founded in 1524, governed them under Castilian law, and Castilian Spanish became the language of most of the Americas. Antonio de Nebrija's <em>Gramática de la lengua castellana</em> of 1492 was the first printed grammar of a modern European vernacular. Under Alfonso X <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#toledo">Toledo</a> became a centre of translation from Arabic, producing the Alfonsine Tables of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a> used across Europe until the sixteenth century, while the king's <em>Cantigas de Santa María</em> are a monument of medieval song. The universities of Salamanca and Alcalá, where Cardinal Francisco Jiménez de Cisneros sponsored the Complutensian Polyglot Bible, nurtured the School of Salamanca, whose theologians such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francisco-de-vitoria">Francisco de Vitoria</a> debated the rights of conquered peoples and helped lay the foundations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#international-law">international law</a>. Castile's Golden Age produced Miguel de Cervantes, Lope de Vega, Teresa of Ávila and Diego Velázquez. Its record also includes the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jews">Jews</a> in 1492, the forced conversion of Muslims and the violence of colonial conquest.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#war-of-the-spanish-succession"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession.jpg" alt="War of the Spanish Succession" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Final_War_of_the_Spanish_Succession_Collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DavidDijkgraaf, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From the accession of Charles I in 1516, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> was the heartland of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy that spanned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a>. Its towns rose against the new king's foreign advisers and tax demands in the Revolt of the Comuneros, crushed at the Battle of Villalar in 1521, after which the Cortes offered little resistance to royal taxation. Castile carried a disproportionate share of the cost of Habsburg wars, and Philip II's government suspended payments to its bankers in 1557, 1560, 1575 and 1596. Plague in 1596–1602, the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moriscos">Moriscos</a> between 1609 and 1614, heavy taxes and debased <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#copper">copper</a> coinage depressed population and trade in the early seventeenth century, while the revolts of Catalonia and Portugal in 1640, provoked by the Count-Duke of Olivares's demands, showed the limits of Castilian leadership. When the childless Charles II died in 1700, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> Philip V to the throne. Because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#valencia">Valencia</a> and Catalonia had largely backed the Habsburg claimant, Philip abolished their separate institutions by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nueva-planta-decrees">Nueva Planta decrees</a> of 1707–1716 and imposed Castilian law and administration. The separate crowns thus merged into a single Spanish monarchy whose language, law and institutions were largely Castilian.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> united Castile and León, conquered most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, sponsored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> and governed the first Spanish empire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a>, giving modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> its dominant language, law and institutions and carrying Castilian Spanish across much of the world.</em></p>

